Here is `POST /v1/bookings` written the quick way, as a handler in `internal/api/bookings.go` that does everything itself:

```go
var (
	mu       sync.Mutex
	bookings []domain.Booking
	nextID   int64 = 1001
)

func createBooking(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req createBookingRequest
	if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "bad JSON", http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}
	in, err := req.validate()
	if err != nil {
		http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusUnprocessableEntity)
		return
	}
	room, ok := rooms[in.RoomID]
	if !ok {
		http.Error(w, "no room has this id", http.StatusUnprocessableEntity)
		return
	}
	if length := in.EndsAt.Sub(in.StartsAt); length < 30*time.Minute || length > 3*time.Hour {
		http.Error(w, "a booking lasts 30 minutes to 3 hours", http.StatusUnprocessableEntity)
		return
	}
	if !in.StartsAt.After(time.Now()) {
		http.Error(w, "must start in the future", http.StatusUnprocessableEntity)
		return
	}
	if in.Attendees > room.Capacity {
		http.Error(w, room.Name+" is too small", http.StatusUnprocessableEntity)
		return
	}
	mu.Lock()
	defer mu.Unlock()
	for _, b := range bookings {
		if b.RoomID == in.RoomID && b.StartsAt.Before(in.EndsAt) && in.StartsAt.Before(b.EndsAt) {
			http.Error(w, "room already booked", http.StatusConflict)
			return
		}
	}
	b := domain.Booking{ID: nextID, Status: domain.Confirmed, Version: 1, CreatedAt: time.Now()}
	b.NewBooking = domain.NewBooking{RoomID: in.RoomID, UserID: 1, StartsAt: in.StartsAt,
		EndsAt: in.EndsAt, Attendees: in.Attendees, Note: in.Note}
	nextID++
	bookings = append(bookings, b)
	w.Header().Set("Location", fmt.Sprintf("/v1/bookings/%d", b.ID))
	w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated)
	json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(b)
}
```

It works, and for a weekend project it's fine. But look at how many kinds of work are mixed together in one function: reading HTTP, looking up the room, the booking rules, reading the clock, storing the booking, and shaping the JSON. It even encodes the stored struct as the response, and `domain.Booking` has no JSON tags, so the client gets `RoomID` and an embedded `NewBooking` instead of the contract's camelCase fields, and any field added to the struct later goes to every client. Each kind of work changes for a different reason, and in one function, each change risks the others:

- **The database arrives in the PostgreSQL lesson.** Every line that touches `bookings`, the in-memory list, has to change, and those lines are scattered among the rules.
- **A rule changes.** The library extends bookings to 21 days ahead. You have to find the rule inside HTTP code, and the only way to test it is to send real requests.
- **A second way in.** A nightly job wants to cancel the bookings for a room that's closing, with the same rules. It isn't an HTTP request, so it can't call a handler.
- **Tests.** Checking that a three-and-a-half-hour booking is refused means starting a server, sending JSON, and reading JSON back, for what is really one comparison. And with the real clock inside the handler, a test that passes today fails next week, when its date is in the past.

## Separation of concerns

The fix is an old idea called **separation of concerns**: give each kind of work its own place, so each piece has one reason to change. A **concern** is one kind of work, like HTTP, the business rules, or storage. When the concerns are apart:

- Changing how data is stored touches only the storage code.
- A rule can be tested by calling one function with plain values and a fixed clock.
- The nightly job calls the same rule code the handler does.
- A newcomer knows where to look: HTTP problems in one place, rule bugs in another, slow queries in a third.

The cost is more files, and a little code to connect them. For an API that will live for years and be changed by many people, that cost pays for itself the first week. The rest of this lesson splits Roombook along exactly these lines, and every later lesson builds on the split.

The model splits along the line part 1 drew: rules on one side, storage on the other.

- A **service** holds the business rules for one area, like bookings. It's the code that answers "may Ada book Turing on Monday from 14:00 to 15:30?", and makes the booking if so. Once Roombook has a database, it has a second job: deciding which changes must happen together, in one database **transaction**, so they all happen or none do.
- A **repository** holds the data access for one kind of thing. It's the only code that knows where bookings live: today a list in memory, and a PostgreSQL table once the database lessons arrive. That makes it the only code with SQL in it. Its methods are named in the domain's words, like `insert`, `findById`, and `countUpcoming`, not in the storage's.

With the controller from the last part, that makes three layers:

```diagram layers-and-mvc-layers
The layers call downward only. The model's types are shared by all three.
```

## Dependencies point one way

The arrows in the diagram only point down. The controller calls the service, the service calls the repository, and never the other way around. A service never reads a request or picks a status code, and a repository never applies a business rule. A layer **depends on** another when its code uses it, and this one-way rule about dependencies is what lets you replace a layer. As long as the repository keeps its methods, the service can't tell a list in memory from a database.

To make that real in code, the service doesn't depend on one particular repository. It depends on an **interface**, a description of the methods it needs, and any repository with those methods will do.

In Go, the interface belongs to the code that *uses* it, so the service defines what it needs, in `internal/service/bookings.go`:

```go
type BookingStore interface {
	Get(ctx context.Context, id int64) (domain.Booking, error)
	Insert(ctx context.Context, b domain.NewBooking) (domain.Booking, error)
	CountUpcoming(ctx context.Context, userID int64, now time.Time) (int, error)
}

type RoomStore interface {
	Get(ctx context.Context, id int64) (domain.Room, error)
}

var errBookingLimit = &domain.ProblemError{Status: http.StatusConflict,
	Type: "booking-limit", Detail: "You already have 3 upcoming bookings."}

type BookingService struct {
	bookings BookingStore
	rooms    RoomStore
	now      func() time.Time
}

func NewBookingService(bookings BookingStore, rooms RoomStore, now func() time.Time) *BookingService {
	return &BookingService{bookings: bookings, rooms: rooms, now: now}
}
```

Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly: any type with these methods is a `BookingStore`, without saying so. So the store, in `internal/store/memory`, never imports the service, and the service never imports the store. The PostgreSQL lesson's `postgres.NewBookingStore(pool)` satisfies the same interface with SQL inside, and adds `ListForUser` and `Cancel` for listing a member's bookings and cancelling one. The in-memory stores from the routes lesson now take a context and return an error, the way a database store will. Here is the in-memory booking store's `Insert`, in `bookings.go`:

```go
type BookingStore struct {
	mu     sync.Mutex
	now    func() time.Time
	rows   map[int64]domain.Booking
	nextID int64
}

func NewBookingStore(now func() time.Time) *BookingStore {
	return &BookingStore{now: now, rows: map[int64]domain.Booking{}, nextID: 1001}
}

func (s *BookingStore) Insert(ctx context.Context, b domain.NewBooking) (domain.Booking, error) {
	s.mu.Lock()
	defer s.mu.Unlock()
	for _, o := range s.rows {
		if o.Status == domain.Confirmed && o.RoomID == b.RoomID &&
			o.StartsAt.Before(b.EndsAt) && b.StartsAt.Before(o.EndsAt) {
			return domain.Booking{}, &domain.ProblemError{Status: http.StatusConflict,
				Type: "booking-overlap", Detail: "The room is already booked then."}
		}
	}
	row := domain.Booking{NewBooking: b, ID: s.nextID, Status: domain.Confirmed, Version: 1, CreatedAt: s.now()}
	s.rows[row.ID] = row
	s.nextID++
	return row, nil
}
```

A mutex guards the map, since many requests run at once, each on its own goroutine. The overlap check stands in for the database constraint that the lesson on constraints and transactions builds, and `s.now()` stamps `CreatedAt`, the way a database fills in `now()`. `now` is a dependency of the service too: reading the clock is like any other outside input, so a test can pass a fixed time, and Roombook passes the course's fixed clock. The rule to remember is *accept interfaces, return structs*: `NewBookingService` takes interfaces, so callers can hand it any store, and returns a concrete `*BookingService`, so callers get every method.

## DTOs at the edges

The types that cross the HTTP boundary are called **DTOs**, short for data transfer objects: the parsed request body coming in, from the validation lesson, and the view going out. They're kept apart from the model on purpose. The create DTO has no `id`, `status`, or `createdAt`, because the client mustn't set them. The view has no internal fields, because the client mustn't see them. And the model can gain a field, like a flag for a booking that staff created, without it leaking into a single response or becoming something a client can send.
